1. e4! e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 (3... Nf6 4. d3 (4. Nc3)) 4. d3 { As a first step, rather than precise variations to memorize, it will be
very important to understand the three themes that often come up in this
Italian with d2-d3:
• Early kingside castling (even sometimes downright premature);
• The pin of the ♘f6 (or ♘f3) after, respectively, ♗g5 or …♗g4;
• The move h2-h3 (or …h7-h6, respectively), before or after castling, to
counter the aforementioned pin. } 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 (5. O-O d6 { Early castling before d6 is ok } 6. a3 a6 7. b4 Ba7 8. Be3 O-O) 5... d6 (5... O-O 6. Bg5 Be7) 6. Bg5 h6 7. Bxf6 { Tabja to play } *


