1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. d3 O-O?! { This time it is Black who is compromised by castling too early and
allowing the pin of the f6 knight. } 6. Bg5 d6? { Black was able to benefit from the fact that he had not yet played …d7-d6
by returning to e7 with the ♗c5, but of course, in doing so he would have
offered a free tempo to White, which is never negligible at such an early stage of the opening. } 7. Nd5 Bg4 8. Qd2!! { An idea already glimpsed in the previous game, with colours reversed. The
first exclamation mark is for the strength of the move, the second is for
its originality, since in this position White usually takes on f6 with the knight. } (8. Nxf6+ gxf6 9. Bh6 Re8 10. h3 Be6) 8... Bh5 (8... Bxf3 9. Bxf6 gxf6 (9... Qd7 10. Bxg7! Kxg7 11. Qg5+ Kh8 12. Qf6+ Kg8 13. gxf3) 10. Qh6 Bxg2 11. Rg1) (8... Be6 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Qh6 Bxd5 11. Bxd5 Nd4 12. h4! Nxc2+? 13. Kd1 Nxa1 14. Ng5!!) 9. Bxf6! gxf6 10. Qh6 Bg6 11. h4 Qd7 12. Nxf6+ Kh8 13. Ng5 Nd4 14. Ngxh7 Nf5 15. exf5 Qxf5 16. Nxf8+ Bh7 17. Bxf7 Qxf2+ 18. Kd1 Qf5 19. Be6 Be3 20. Bxf5 Bxh6 21. N8xh7 { 1-0 Black resigns. } 1-0


